{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-81025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4{\\text{i}}^{2}-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-4\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-81025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\lambda =-2\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =-4\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =2\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =4\\text{i}"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\lambda =-4\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\text{i} \\\\ 4-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\text{i} \\\\ -4{\\text{i}}^{2}-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=2\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-38281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\lambda =2"] ["math" "\\lambda =2+2\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =2\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =\\text{i}"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\lambda =2\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\text{i} \\\\ 6-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\text{i} \\\\ -6{\\text{i}}^{2}-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=3\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =3\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\lambda =-3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =3"] ["math" "\\lambda =3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =\\text{i}"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\lambda =3\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-39181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-39181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-49-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}49{\\text{i}}^{2}-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=7\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-39181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-39181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-39181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\lambda =-13\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =-7\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =13\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =7\\text{i}"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-39181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\lambda =7\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{\\text{i}}^{2}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i}(\\text{i}+1) \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(1+\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1+\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\lambda =-\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =1-\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =\\text{i}"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}{-1}=\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-38287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "2+4\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\text{i}-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}-1"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1+3\\text{i}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "1-3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{i}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\text{i}}{1}=\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-81185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\text{i}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "1+\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-3-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-5}{3-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-5(3+\\text{i})}{10}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-81027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}+\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-2}{2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-2(2-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+4\\text{i})(-2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-81195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5-5\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 10 & 5-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-5-5\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}+(5-5\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+5\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1-5\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}{(-2)(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-3\\text{i})(1+3\\text{i})}{(5)(5)}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{10}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{10}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-(-5)(5) & =0 \\\\ -15-5\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(10)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{-36}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 6\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm 3\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+3\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(4-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1-3\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4-3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(4+3\\text{i})(4-3\\text{i})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ -12+6\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-4(1)(8)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+2\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-4-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(4-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2-2\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(2)(2)}{(-2-\\text{i})(-2+\\text{i})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0aed9f2d60c3511e84d5ff8866713509b9d5a365e5d992dfbf6afbb654d9a514"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1e5fd5044910a995f5fd618a15c2ab22542961f3bf9a7d50184f6ad1dd3906d"} {"question" "e-5528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine whether ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/59af73d1cda1a988c775ccf198b53653b96660b4ad8416add1b0c1816e89a5a5.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/963bcaf94bc481fe8fbc99ca28fba037047d8821661eadcf0e5e9ce50ba39ef8.png)\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/example-5528.json" "file_sha256" "33136c9832c49911d019177d35e83a4bc5c0f171fb05c99e046303807a83e310"} {"question" "e-5528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v} = 6\\textrm{i} \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=6\\textrm{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/example-5528.json" "file_sha256" "33136c9832c49911d019177d35e83a4bc5c0f171fb05c99e046303807a83e310"} {"question" "e-5527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/7dad070080562e459c926f5c8fa3d71a56981d68bd3ed4aae768c2f7450685a3.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/example-5527.json" "file_sha256" "6d6dbd1bb174d9b8502765885f3b021c9aaf1bba5a727d4a96ccb64d588e2be0"} {"question" "e-5527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/6786c7bc4d12bc3ee1a335eeb45967475850a585ec36805fe81aa76360d5a729.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/a73682dbe1dfccc14e5b3f0c68aa6e6aa7eb7a67bb475efbbaeaa1e886585554.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/example-5527.json" "file_sha256" "6d6dbd1bb174d9b8502765885f3b021c9aaf1bba5a727d4a96ccb64d588e2be0"} {"question" "e-5529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/e6a0d43572790d4daa49659c271c17032f2601458db0f6843257df07c245f372.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/9bf515d20e5a19646680474f0243dba793d1383eade4bf4986c7b11501baa800.png) be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/1afe535b20d0fd1ce03b2b0886424a5ef1a29f4eea7e47c1aa818af67c98e868.png)\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/example-5529.json" "file_sha256" "b7ae578420bc9508b0c2ff386d5ea0c115908f3c62ae595c2a29948087af4fc8"} {"question" "e-5529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-5\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/af8f52f23a6fdcd1f070eff5876415cc884ffaafcbb2fe3f8737c31cc4761c86.png)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/65c073a60a27234349281e873958c2c9d54fb703331a4de23cc358a3eab1b232.png) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/ca11ae0cca8d07c026108e71b96398171597f7330cb2390d871a8071e7bba873.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/example-5529.json" "file_sha256" "b7ae578420bc9508b0c2ff386d5ea0c115908f3c62ae595c2a29948087af4fc8"}] "usage" {"e-5528" [] "e-5527" [] "q-38475" [] "q-38480" [] "e-5529" []} "basis" 1520 "retractions" [[17592186176393 :question/problem "Determine whether $v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 - 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?"] [17592186176396 :question/problem "Given that $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{1} \\\\ b_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{2} \\\\ b_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{a_{1}a_{2}}{b_{1}b_{2}}$?"] [17592186175702 :question/problem "Determine whether $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?"] [17592186175702 :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAv &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-18 + 6\\textrm{i} \\\\ 24\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}18\\textrm{i}^{2} + 6\\textrm{i} \\\\ 24\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6\\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6\\textrm{i}v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v} = 6\\textrm{i} \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda=6\\textrm{i}\n$$"] [17592186175701 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}$."] [17592186175701 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-3 + 4\\textrm{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 + 4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4\\textrm{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{-4\\textrm{i}x_{1} + 4x_{2} = 0; - 4x_{1} - 4\\textrm{i}x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186175703 :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{1} \\\\ b_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{2} \\\\ b_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}$."] [17592186175703 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λ_{1}I &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 + 5\\textrm{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 + 5\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-5\\textrm{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{-5\\textrm{i}x_{1} + 5x_{2} = 0; - 5x_{1} - 5\\textrm{i}x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ \\textrm{i}x_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_1=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis a complex eigenvalue of $A$, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [1759218